window.docContents[207].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--From-classical-propagation-to-weak-selection":{"contents":"The first route moves from transport to waves:\n\n\n\nThe nonlinear first-order route moves from smooth characteristics to\nnonsmooth solution concepts:\n\n\n\nThe variational route builds weak waves by Galerkin approximation, proves\nenergy uniqueness, and then puts differentiated time estimates together with\nelliptic spatial lifts to get higher regularity. The semigroup section\nsupplies the abstract language for linear evolution generated by an\noperator.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","header":"7.1.3. From classical propagation to weak selection","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--From-classical-propagation-to-weak-selection"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Scalar-Theory--Large-times":{"contents":"The N-wave comparison profile has expanding negative and positive lobes,\nseparated from the zero state by shocks. A zero lobe parameter removes that\nlobe. The parameters come from extrema of the initial primitive, rather than\nfrom the separate integrals of the positive and negative parts of the\ncompactly supported datum. For Burgers's flux the comparison profile is\n\n\n\nFor a general flux the fan is centred at  and its slope and width\nare scaled by .\n\nLet  be  with  and , and let\n vanish almost everywhere outside a bounded interval, with\nboth lobe masses  and  positive. Then there is a constant  such\nthat, for the Lax–Oleinik solution ,\n\nWrite . For large , the solution vanishes outside the two\nfronts  and , up to a\nbounded distance. On an interior interval of length  the footpoint\nstays in the bounded support and the slope differs from the linear profile\n by , using a third-order expansion of the\nflux at . Two strips of bounded width near the fronts, where both\nfunctions are , contribute the leading error . For\nbounded  the bound follows from  and the finite\npropagation of the support.\n\n\n\nThe figure rescales so that the N-wave becomes a single fixed shape. The\nleft panel shows  against , and the profiles\nsettle onto the rescaled N-wave. The right panel is the  error on a\nlog scale; it follows the slope  of the theorem. The datum is placed\nso that the minimum of  sits away from the jump, which is what\nmakes the rate  sharp rather than a crude upper bound for this\nexample.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws\u0009The Scalar Theory","header":"7.5.2.8.3. Large times","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--The-Scalar-Theory--Large-times"},"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--What-a-partial-differential-equation-asks":{"contents":"A differential equation relates an unknown function to its derivatives, and a\npartial differential equation has several independent variables. Schematically\na PDE is a relation\n\non a domain . It may be linear or nonlinear in  and its\nderivatives, stationary or evolutionary. The notation compares values at a\npoint. Yet the questions we care about concern a whole domain or an entire\nevolution. For the rod, holding the ends at zero lets heat escape; insulating\nthem imposes a zero normal derivative and conserves total heat. The interior\nequation is the same.\n\nThe heat equation , the wave equation\n, and the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations\n\nall need this surrounding information. A domain, function spaces, boundary\nand initial data, and a meaning for the derivatives turn a differential\nidentity into a PDE problem.\n\nFor Poisson's equation, the choice of boundary data appears directly in the\nproblem statement:\n\nThe statement still leaves us work to do. Does  mean an ordinary\nrestriction of a continuous function, or a Sobolev trace? Is  continuous,\nsquare-integrable, or a distribution? The answers determine whether we seek\npointwise second derivatives or an integral identity. They also determine\nwhich estimates are available. An interior equation alone leaves the boundary\nvalue free, just as the heat residual alone did not choose the rod's ends.\n\nHadamard's well-posedness asks for existence, uniqueness, and continuous\ndependence on the data. Existence says there is a solution in our chosen class.\nUniqueness says the same data cannot produce two solutions in that class.\nContinuous dependence says that a small change in the data produces a small\nchange in the solution, measured in specified norms. This last requirement\nis what makes an approximation to the data useful.\n\nWe can see it in the rod without leaving the sine functions. Add a small,\nfiner oscillation to the initial temperature:\n\nLinearity gives the corresponding solution\n\nSubtracting the original solution  leaves just the\nperturbation. Its size in the  norm is\n\nHere the  norm measures the square root of the integrated squared\ntemperature error. Heat flow damps that error; the fine oscillation disappears\neven faster than the original profile. The formula constructs solutions for\nthese particular data. The energy argument below will explain why the\nsame stability estimate applies much more generally, and why it also gives\nuniqueness when the initial difference is zero.\n\n\n\nTrying to recover the past temperature reverses this advantage. Fix .\nThe final profiles  tend to zero in  as\n, but their initial profiles  all have norm\n. Arbitrarily small errors at the final time can therefore\nhide a substantial initial oscillation. Even on these smooth, exactly\nsolvable data, the inverse heat map is not continuous in .\nWell-posedness belongs to an equation together with its data, direction\nof evolution, solution class, and topology.\n\nFor linear second-order equations, the principal part explains much of this\nbehavior. Elliptic equations describe equilibrium. Harmonic functions obey\nmean-value identities and maximum principles, so boundary values constrain\nthe interior. Divergence-form operators\n\nare particularly suited to integration by parts: their weak formulations\nexpose bilinear forms whose coercivity can give existence and uniqueness.\nParabolic equations describe diffusion. The decay of the higher sine modes is\none manifestation of smoothing; spreading a localized pulse with a Gaussian\nis another. Hyperbolic equations describe propagation along characteristics,\nwith finite speed in the wave equation.\n\n\n\nThe three panels show a steady harmonic saddle, a spreading heat pulse, and a\ntraveling wave. The distinction matters when choosing a proof: a maximum\nprinciple controls an equilibrium through its boundary, a diffusion estimate\ncontrols smoothing, and characteristics follow information as it moves.\nNonlinearity can change the solution class itself. A smooth initial profile\nfor a conservation law may develop a shock. The distributional equation\ncontinues to make sense, but entropy inequalities are needed to select an\nadmissible solution. Hamilton–Jacobi equations instead use viscosity\nsolutions, defined through comparison with touching test functions.\n\nFluid mechanics brings these methods together. Euler describes inviscid\ntransport constrained by incompressibility; Navier–Stokes adds viscous\ndiffusion. Pressure is recovered through an elliptic equation even though\nvelocity evolves in time. Leray's global weak solutions to three-dimensional\nNavier–Stokes supply energy control in a weak solution class. Classical\nregularity and uniqueness require further arguments in a specified class.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.1. What a partial differential equation asks","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--What-a-partial-differential-equation-asks"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Interface-with-Morrey-Embedding":{"contents":"The bounded Morrey embedding operator lands in C0GammaOn; composing with\nthe observation maps gives pointwise bounds, while restrictCLM transfers a\nglobal representative to a domain. A separate almost-everywhere theorem\nrelates the selected continuous representative to the original Sobolev base\ncoordinate.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Hölder Spaces","header":"4.3.3.4. Interface with Morrey Embedding","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Interface-with-Morrey-Embedding"}});